  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/ad/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_101.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Thor is stomping through the city in a terrible mood -- he <i>has </i>to see Odin, and passersby on the streets are terrified at his grim countenance.&nbsp; He even calls out for the \"mortals\" to get away from him and declares he has lost interest in your \"petty, puny lives!\"\n\nSome nearby ants notify Hank Pym, and he (as Giant-Man) and the Wasp summon Iron Man to head out and see why Thor is in such a foul mood.&nbsp; He's paying no attention to the damage he's causing along the way!&nbsp; \"Mortals!&nbsp; How like chattering monkeys you are!\"&nbsp; He's in a <i>frightful</i> mood!\n\nAfter smashing a truck that was bearing down on him (because he wasn't watching where he was walking), he angrily turns to the Avengers and tells them that what he has to do must be done alone!&nbsp; \"This is no concern of yours!&nbsp; I say <i>go</i>!\"&nbsp; Iron Man convinces Giant-Man that this appears to be a personal problem and that it's none of their business.&nbsp; The other Avengers hesitantly leave him in peace.\n\nThor is still miffed about Odin's declaration about Jane at the end of the previous issue, that he will not grant him permission to marry Jane Foster!&nbsp; He has had Loki assist him in hearing his favorite son's thoughts and learns that Thor is still pining over the mortal woman even though Odin has forbidden it.&nbsp; Loki even poses the question (to Odin), \"Is this not rank disobedience?\"&nbsp; Odin <i>agrees</i>, even to the point of pronouncing a <i>punishment</i> upon Thor, reducing his power by <i>half</i>.&nbsp; Furthermore, Thor will no longer be able to \"control the storm and the elements!!&nbsp; And no longer may he come to Asgard!&nbsp; Not until he gives up all thoughts of the mortal girl he loves!\"\n\nWhen Thor approaches Heimdall for entrance into Asgard, he is <i>refused</i>!&nbsp; \"Those are the orders of Odin himself!\"&nbsp; Thor will hear none of <i>this</i> and wields his hammer to strike Heimdall, but with his power reduced, Heimdall is able to withstand his attack and even strike back!&nbsp; Thor is surprised and tries again before realizing that Heimdall is right.&nbsp; His power <i>has</i> been halved!&nbsp; He cries out to his father, but to no avail!\n\nLoki then realizes that this is his best chance to strike, while Thor is at a disadvantage.&nbsp; But he knows it can't be a <i>direct</i> strike, or Odin will know.&nbsp; He must use someone else as an <i>agent</i> through which he can strike Thor.&nbsp; He commands the \"Norn Hag\" (Haag) to show him the \"Well of Centuries\" and searches the timeline, eventually finding Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man in Earth's \"far distant future.\"&nbsp; After the events of [LINK:1962:JIM_86_thor0|JIM #86|2026-05-01], Thor took away his memory of their encounter, but Loki restores it.\n\nZarrko is incensed that he suddenly finds himself working a menial job (all because of Thor's interference) and finds a way back to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, in order to retrieve a bomb (which no longer exists in his time), this time taking a modified mining robot with him.&nbsp; When he arrives in the 20<sup>th</sup> century, the robot turns the police guns to dust!&nbsp; He raises a ruckus in town to attract Thor, and sure enough, Don Blake sees it on the news and hurries off to meet him as Thor, even knowing that his strength has been reduced by half.\n\nHe sees the robot tossing a building down on the road to block it, but when Thor crashes his hammer down to bash it into pieces, it doesn't break up!&nbsp; His power has <i>definitely</i> been reduced!&nbsp; He tussles with the robot and holds his own for a while before he finally is hurled backward.&nbsp; Zarrko offers to let him return to the 23<sup>rd</sup> century and serve him rather than let the robot go on a rampage, destroying the city!\n\nThor decides that if <i>he</i> can't defeat the robot with only half his strength, <i>nobody</i> can, and he agrees to Zarrko's terms.&nbsp; Zarrko is thrilled.&nbsp; He'll now have Thor helping him take over the 23<sup>rd</sup> century!&nbsp; They leave together.\n\nMeanwhile, back on Asgard, Odin and Loki watch, and not realizing <i>why</i> Thor has left the 20<sup>th</sup> century, Odin thinks that Thor has surrendered to a mortal!&nbsp; Loki is triumphant because if Thor helps Zarrko in the 23<sup>rd</sup> century, then Odin will never forgive him, never realizing that Loki was behind <i>all</i> of it!\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "A half-powered Thor makes a bad situation worse, and Loki couldn't be happier.\nA few stumbles keep it from greatness, but the cliffhanger lands.",
  "subtitle": "An Asgardian Performance Review",
  "review_text": "A quite solid issue, which came as a bit of a shock at first when I saw the cover and realized that the Tomorrow Man would be returning, since my recollection of him was that he was pretty lame.&nbsp; Except, <i>heh</i>, right off the bat, I got a laugh after seeing the cover with the guy in the lower-left corner, showing an in-your-face horror look, fleeing the scene in terror!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>Hahahaha</i>!&nbsp; Such <i>classic </i>Kirby!]\n\nThor's mood at the beginning was a <i>bit</i> of a problem.&nbsp; He was miffed -- I <i>get</i> it -- but to be so single-minded as to allow himself to become a rampaging madman through the city -- is it any <i>wonder</i> the Avengers felt the need to get out there to try and talk some sense into him?\n\nThat was <i>good</i>, but I felt they gave up too soon.\n[ASSET: center | NoConcernOfYours | width=67%]\n\nThor's words to them on page 3 that \"This is no concern of yours!\" may well be true, but I would imagine that if one goes around smashing things and generally causing wanton mayhem, that <i>makes</i> it Avengers business (at least in my mind)!\n\nAgain, <b>I <i>get</i> it</b> -- there are only so many pages you can use, but that seemed a bit much, [SPAN: severity=error; text=and it prevented the book from grading higher.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nPage 4 shows Odin reducing Thor's power by half and removing his ability to control the storm and elements.&nbsp; On the surface, it feels like [SPAN: severity=warning; text=a <i>colossal</i> overreaction,] and I question the need for his interference.&nbsp; He'd already <i>spoken</i> and declared that the mortal Jane Foster was unsuitable for him -- why add more punishment?\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=I question this course of action!]\n[BR] But other than this, Odin, Loki, and Heimdall were <i>all</i> completely in character, [SPAN: severity=success; text=and in that regard, they were great!]\n\n[ASSET: left | PowerHalved | width=16%] After being refused entry into Asgard by Heimdall -- and finding himself unable to overpower the guardian of Bifrost, Thor realizes that he is being punished, and his look of anguish after Odin refuses to respond to his pleas is a bit heart-rending.\n[BR]\n[BR] Seeing him wrestle with this reduction in power was good.\n\nDid he perhaps give up too quickly?&nbsp; <i>Sure</i>.\n[BR] Was this perhaps due to lack of space?&nbsp; <i>Probably.</i>\n\nOne might wonder why he didn't return to his Avengers buddies and request help.&nbsp; While this may be a valid question, Thor had already told them on page 3 that the entire matter was [SPAN: severity=info; text=none of their concern.]&nbsp; Perhaps he felt that involving them in matters between the gods was out of their league, or it might have simply been that he was too <i>embarrassed</i> by his sudden loss in power and didn't want them to know.\n\nThe story doesn't say, [SPAN: severity=success; text=but the fact that the reader is left to wonder about this is a good thing.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | LokiPlots | width=25%] Naturally, Loki being who he is, you <i>knew</i> he was going to take advantage of the situation, and to <b>no one's</b> surprise, he journeys to the Well of Centuries to track down <i>someone</i> to menace the Thunder God on his behalf (because I guess he felt Odin was keeping an annoyingly close eye on him).\n[BR]\n[BR] Recall also that Loki stated in his <i>previous</i> appearance (in [LINK:1963:JIM_97_thor0|JIM #97|2026-12-16], page 5) that he was a \"prisoner of Asgard, unable to leave!\"&nbsp; So finding another \"catspaw\" is consistent with his earlier portrayal.\n\n[ASSET: right | ZarrkoMemoryRestored | width=11%] And so it is, that at the story's halfway point (middle of page 7), the villain depicted on the cover finally appears:&nbsp; Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man, returns!&nbsp; Readers were first introduced to him over a year earlier, back in [LINK:1962:JIM_86_thor0|JIM #86|2026-05-01], and the narrative pretty much shifts its focus entirely to him at this midway point.\n[BR]\n[Br] It's probably worth noting that at no time does Zarrko ever realize that he's being helped by Loki, who was responsible for restoring his memory (that he lost during that earlier encounter with Thor).\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | HammerIneffective | width=16%] [ASSET: right | ThorConcedes | width=18%] The rest of the story unfolds about as you'd expect, with Zarrko returning to the 20<sup>th</sup> century to retrieve a weapon, with which he can return to his own time and terrorize the populace there -- except this time he comes with a robot to assist.\n[BR]\n[BR] Thor heads out to stop him and finds that he's no longer up to the task.\n[BR]\n[BR] Believing that if he can't stop Zarrko's robot, <i>nobody </i>can, Thor's strategy shifts to simply getting him out of the 20<sup>th</sup> century and back to his own time, promising to return with him to the 23<sup>rd</sup> century and there, \"do your bidding.\"\n\n[ASSET: left | TriumphantLoki | width=9%] But to the still-observing Odin, it appears as if his own son has surrendered to a mortal!\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Outrageous!]\n[BR]\n[BR] Loki's look of triumph is [SPAN: severity=success; text=excellent], and the book closes with its second cliffhanger ending!\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Don't dare miss the conclusion!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs for the artwork, the inking for this issue over Kirby's pencils by \"G. Bell\" is starkly different, with more emphasis on thin lines around the face.&nbsp; Still obviously Kirby, but it is interesting how different inking affects the overall look.\n\nJack's page 5 depiction of the rainbow bridge (Bifrost) -- with <i>seventeen </i>different color stripes -- well <i>okay</i>, that was a bit overdone (especially since on the very next panel, there are suddenly only eight), but... let's just chalk that up to artistic license and not nitpick, shall we?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But <i>still</i>... <b>seventeen?</b>)]\n\nLastly (and I realize this isn't artwork), I loved Jan's thought bubble at the bottom of page 2, where Giant-Man says to her, \"Now follow me!\"&nbsp; To which, she thinks, \"To the ends of the Earth, you big dream-boat!\"\n\nStan could easily have omitted that, especially since this is a story about <i>Thor</i>, but he faithfully kept her in character, even in this almost throwaway panel.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=It may be a small thing], but world-building -- even during guest appearances -- makes the magazines worth reading (and it doesn't hurt that Stan is the writer of all the main universe titles anyway, <i>heh</i>, making it easy for him to scatter these nuggets about).\n\nAnd, as for Jan, despite her near-constant flirting with the other male Avengers (particularly Thor), this small panel shows where her true feelings lie.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nWhile Zarrko may not have been a great choice for pitting against Thor, I really like the way the book so confidently concluded with its second consecutive cliffhanger ending (\"consecutive\" in the sense that [LINK:1963:JIM_99_thor0|JIM #99|2027-02-06] continued into [LINK:1963:JIM_100_thor0|JIM #100|2027-03-04], and now we do it again).&nbsp; This shows a level of trust in the readership -- that they will indeed <i>return</i> next month.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=That's progress!]\n\nOdin's decision to remove half of Thor's powers [SPAN: severity=error; text=may seem harsh], but at this early stage, we still don't know a ton about him.&nbsp; In some prior stories, he's had Loki bound up in Asgard, while in others, Loki appears free to move around the realm, so his punishments are still a mystery, and we need more time to learn about how the All-Father operates.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I am inclined to give Odin that time,] so in that regard, for at least <i>this</i> month, I will not penalize his actions here.\n\nThat being said, I can [SPAN: severity=warning; text=still question] why he ordered Loki to reveal Thor's thoughts on page 4, but <b>not</b> on page 13.&nbsp; He could easily have learned <i>why</i> Thor seemingly surrendered to a mortal.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=It's a definite stumble on the part of the plot.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIn the end, the good outweighs the negatives.&nbsp; Even though the Avengers were underutilized, it was nice to see them appear, and Thor's despair over his father's refusal to grant him an audience was surprisingly moving.&nbsp; Loki's behind-the-scenes manipulation was perfectly in character, the cover look of horror was funny, and even though Zarrko isn't the greatest opponent for Thor, the trust the book places in its readership is refreshing.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=The stumbles were real, and ultimately prevented a higher rating,] but overall, [SPAN: severity=success; text=this was a surprisingly solid issue (or <i>half</i> issue at least),] and I found myself turning the pages eagerly!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Readers walk away wondering what will happen next, and that's never a bad thing.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] Loki states on page 7 that Zarrko is from \"Earth's far distant future.\"&nbsp; First off, he doesn't refer to Earth as Midgard, but more importantly, Zarrko was shown back in [LINK:1962:JIM_86_thor0|JIM #86|2026-05-01] to be only three centuries in the future (in the year 2262, now presumably 2263).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<i>That's</i> \"far distant?\"&nbsp; <i>Hmm...</i>]\n\n[HANG0] The back-up story called \"The Enemies\" features a race called the \"Vulcans,\" a \"brutal race of ruthless warriors!\"&nbsp; What's ironic is that they face a race called the \"Idylicans,\" and it's <b><i>they</i></b> who have the pointed ears!&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>...",
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:&nbsp; </b>\"The Invasion of Asgard\"\n\nThis is more of the same as the last issue, as Loki tries to prevent the boyhood Thor from doing valiant deeds that allow him to slowly gain enough strength to finally lift the hammer of Odin.&nbsp; Loki is clearly more malevolent in this issue, but in the end, Thor is rescued by Odin's forces and is victorious, despite the undiscovered treachery of his brother."
  ],
  "highlight": "Odin punishes Thor, Loki exploits it, Zarrko benefits, and the Avengers discover that \"none of your concern\" apparently means \"go home.\""
}